## Research Question

For a tree T on n vertices, let n2(T) denote the number of degree-2 vertices, and let b(T) denote the exact burning number.

We are investigating the conditional statement

```
n2(T) <= 2 ceil(sqrt(n)) - 2
    implies
b(T) <= ceil(sqrt(n)).
```

A genuine counterexample must satisfy ALL of the following for its own value of n:

```
q = ceil(sqrt(n))

n2(T) <= 2q - 2

b(T) >= q + 1
```

Equivalently, search for trees satisfying

```
n2(T) <= 2 ceil(sqrt(n)) - 2
```

and

```
b(T) > ceil(sqrt(n)).
```

IMPORTANT:

q is NOT an independent parameter.

For every tree, q MUST be computed from its actual order n:

```
q = ceil(sqrt(n)).
```

Do NOT classify a tree using a manually chosen q.

## Burning Number Algorithm

Use EXACTLY the same validated burning-number algorithm used in all previous experiments.

DO NOT modify the algorithm.

DO NOT replace it with another implementation.

DO NOT change the burning-number definition.

The burning process is:

```
B_0 = empty set.
```

At step t, choose a source vertex v_t not already burned, and update

```
B_t = B_{t-1} union N(B_{t-1}) union {v_t}.
```

The burning number b(T) is the minimum number of steps needed to burn all vertices.

Use the same exact memoization/search procedure as in the validated program from the previous experiments.

